# A root module with a name a subdirectory also uses — deliberately.
#
# **This file exists to pin the sharp edge of the rule, in a program that
# runs** (panels 099 R1, 100 R5, 101 R5). `render/ascii.hero` says
# `use scale`, and there is a `render/scale.hero` sitting right beside it.
# The `use` reaches THIS file, because every `use` starts at the directory of
# the file you compile — never beside the file that writes the line.
#
# It is correct and it is silent: no diagnostic fires, because the program is
# exactly what the rule says it is. The loud half exists too — delete this
# file and the same line becomes `unknown_module`, naming the directory it
# looked in — so the compiler teaches the rule the moment it can. What it
# cannot do is guess which file you meant, and a refusal here would leave a
# file inside `render/` with no way at all to name the root's `scale`.
#
# The number is 10 rather than 1 so a wrong resolution changes the program's
# output instead of hiding inside it.

function factor() -> i64
    return 10

test "the root's scale is ten"
    assert factor() == 10
